course: dictionary
lesson: 8626
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and scrub is a good example. The clues are in the words around it, the context. «Teeth are brushed gently in small circles with a soft brush, because hard scrubbing hurts the gums» tells you that scrubbing is a way of cleaning, done hard.
explain: «Nails are kept short and clean, with dirt scrubbed out from under them.» The dirt is being taken away, so scrub means to clean by rubbing hard. Test it: put «cleaned by rubbing hard» into the sentence instead of scrubbed. It still makes sense.
explain: Scrub can also be a thing, not an action. Scrub is land with low bushes and small trees. Scrubs, with an s, are the loose clothes that doctors and nurses wear. Ask yourself: is the word doing something, or is it a thing? That helps you pick the right meaning.
explain: Informal talk adds another meaning. If someone says they will scrub a plan, they mean they will cancel it. If the cleaning meaning does not fit the sentence, try another meaning, and check a dictionary when you are unsure.
fact: Scrub as a verb means to clean something by rubbing it hard, often with a brush, soap and water.
fact: Scrub as a noun means land covered with low bushes and small trees, with little grass.
fact: Scrubs are loose, plain clothes that doctors and nurses wear in hospital.
fact: Informal: to scrub something means to cancel it.
fact: You should scrub your hands for at least 20 seconds with soap to wash off germs.
exercise: «Nails are kept short and clean, with dirt scrubbed out from under them, because germs collect there.» What does scrubbed mean here? | Painted bright | Cut very short | Hidden under a cloth | *Cleaned by rubbing hard
exercise: Ella reads that the scrubland in Australia has low bushes and little grass. What is scrubland? | *Land with low bushes and small trees | A lake with clear, still water | A kind of soft carpet | A big city with tall buildings
exercise: «Doctors and nurses change into their scrubs before they start work.» What are scrubs? | A quick, hard rub on a pan | Brushes for cleaning floors | *Loose, plain clothes worn in hospital | Soap for washing hands
test: Finn «scrubbed the wall clean himself». What did Finn do to the wall? | Knocked it down with a hammer | Painted it a new colour | *Rubbed it hard until it was clean | Hid behind it
test: Mum says, «Let's scrub the trip, it's too cold.» In informal talk, what does scrub mean here? | To clean it with soap and water | To make it longer | *To cancel it | To hide it under bushes
exam: Which meaning of scrub fits best in «Before washing machines, clothes were scrubbed on a washboard»? | To cancel | *To clean by rubbing hard | Low bushes in dry land | Loose clothes worn in hospital
